  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 PM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nRain to Snow Saturday through Monday...The dry conditions will come \nto an end Saturday afternoon (at least for north-central WI) as an \nelongated cold front slowly moves southeast across the region, \nbringing rain. Models have continued to slow the progression of the \nfront/rain, with rain not reaching east-central WI until Sunday \nmorning. As the front/rain exits Sunday afternoon, cold air on the \nbackside will lead to lake-effect snow showers/flurries in far north-\ncentral and far northeast WI, including Door County, Sunday evening \ninto Monday. By Monday morning, rainfall amounts of could exceed \n0.50\", especially for locations north and west of the Fox Valley, \nwhere probabilities are 50-80%. Rainfall amounts closer to 0.25\" are \nmore likely for areas in east-central WI. By Monday evening, \nsnowfall amounts for 0.5\" are focused mainly in Vilas County with \nprobabilities of 15-30% across most of the county and up to 70% \nalong the Upper MI border. It will also be breezy throughout this \ntime frame as strong low-level winds will mix to the surface, mainly \nduring the daytime.\n\nFollowing this system, mainly dry conditions are anticipated through \nthe Thanksgiving holiday.\n\nTemperatures...Southerly flow has aided in warmer air lifting into \nthe region, resulting in temperatures soaring into the upper 50s to \nmid 60s today, or about 20-25 degrees above normal. A couple of \nlocations have already set new record high temperatures for today \n(November 20) as of 1 PM. The well above normal temps continue \ntonight with lows ranging from the upper 40s to low 50s, leading to \na few locations potentially setting new record high minimums tonight \nas well. Given the slower arrival of the cold front, high \ntemperatures will remain well above normal on Saturday with highs \nranging from the upper 50s to mid 60s. Several locations may also be \nflirting with record highs. The arrival of the rain/cold front will \nresult in cooling temperatures into Sunday with highs in the low 40s \nto low 50s. Temperatures will then fall to near normal for the start \nof next work week with highs in the 30s and 40s.",
